{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6ed8eab2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# SEMMA Data Mining Project\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook implements the **SEMMA** (Sample, Explore, Modify, Model, Assess) methodology using the credit card fraud detection dataset.  Although SEMMA was originally developed for SAS Enterprise Miner, its principles are general and emphasise iterative data exploration and model refinement."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6cd0e7d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Sample\n",
    "\n",
    "We draw a stratified sample consisting of 49,000 non‑fraudulent and 1,000 fraudulent transactions.  Sampling accelerates processing and allows us to iterate quickly.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expert Review & Recommendations\n",
    "Sampling must maintain the class distribution to avoid introducing bias. In a full production pipeline, we might employ techniques such as SMOTE for oversampling the minority class or adjust the sample size based on resource constraints."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "89586756",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "# Load data\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('../dataset/creditcard.csv')\n",
    "# Use a stratified sample of 50,000 records to make modelling faster\n",
    "df_major = df[df['Class'] == 0].sample(n=49000, random_state=1)\n",
    "df_minor = df[df['Class'] == 1].sample(n=1000, random_state=1)\n",
    "df_sample = pd.concat([df_major, df_minor]).sample(frac=1, random_state=1)\n",
    "df_sample.reset_index(drop=True, inplace=True)\n",
    "df_sample['Class'].value_counts()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "54cffa63",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Explore\n",
    "\n",
    "Exploratory data analysis examines the distribution of transaction amounts and relationships between variables.  Fraudulent transactions tend to have different distributions in certain principal component spaces, as reflected in the correlation heatmap.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expert Review & Recommendations\n",
    "EDA should probe deeper: visualising pairwise plots, examining time patterns and checking for anomalies.  In the SEMMA philosophy this phase informs subsequent modifications."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a28a93e1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualise the distribution of the 'Amount' feature\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6,4))\n",
    "df_sample['Amount'].hist(bins=50, ax=ax)\n",
    "ax.set_title('Distribution of Transaction Amounts')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Amount')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Frequency')\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Correlation heatmap of a subset of features\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "subset = df_sample[['V1','V2','V3','V4','V5','Amount','Class']]\n",
    "corr = subset.corr()\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6,5))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr, annot=True, fmt='.2f', ax=ax, cmap='coolwarm')\n",
    "plt.title('Correlation Heatmap (Sample)')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "194ef1bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Modify\n",
    "\n",
    "We standardise the features to zero mean and unit variance.  This improves the convergence of many algorithms and ensures that no feature dominates solely due to scale differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expert Review & Recommendations\n",
    "Other modifications could include dimensionality reduction (e.g. t‑SNE for visualisation) or feature selection.  Feature engineering is often the most impactful step in data mining."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "75ed5f60",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Prepare data for modelling\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "X = df_sample.drop(columns=['Class'])\n",
    "y = df_sample['Class']\n",
    "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "X_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n",
    "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X_scaled, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=1, stratify=y)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8b756d3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Model\n",
    "\n",
    "We employ a random forest classifier, which handles nonlinearities and interactions naturally.  Using 100 trees strikes a balance between performance and computational cost.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expert Review & Recommendations\n",
    "Hyperparameter tuning (e.g. adjusting tree depth, minimum samples per leaf) and exploring alternative models (XGBoost, LightGBM) are recommended for production systems."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "79449d3f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Train a random forest classifier\n",
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
    "clf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, n_jobs=-1)\n",
    "clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import precision_score, recall_score, f1_score, roc_auc_score\n",
    "prec = precision_score(y_test, y_pred)\n",
    "rec = recall_score(y_test, y_pred)\n",
    "f1 = f1_score(y_test, y_pred)\n",
    "roc_auc = roc_auc_score(y_test, clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:,1])\n",
    "print(f'Precision: {prec:.4f}, Recall: {rec:.4f}, F1: {f1:.4f}, ROC AUC: {roc_auc:.4f}')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c60f788a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Assess\n",
    "\n",
    "The random forest achieves high ROC AUC but precision and recall reveal the difficulty of catching rare events.  A confusion matrix inspection would show many false positives and some false negatives.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expert Review & Recommendations\n",
    "Assessment should compare multiple models and incorporate business costs.  For example, missing a fraud (false negative) may cost more than investigating a false alert (false positive).  Cost‑sensitive learning can directly optimise for such trade‑offs."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "metadata": {},
